# Agent Sessions

Practical guide to bootstrapping and maintaining a session in this
workspace. If the PreToolUse hook is blocking your tools, this is the
skill you need.

See also: `agent-lifecycle` skill for the full lifecycle model
(phases, chat integration, shutdown protocol).

---

## The Bootstrap Sequence

Every primary agent in a fresh VS Code chat window starts from a top-level
shell that already has `HOLLY_SESSION_ID`. Treat it as always available in that
top-level shell:

```bash
holly doctor              # run directly, not through context-mode
eval "$(holly task start --id <issue-id> --intent '<agent-intent>')"
# Each user prompt re-arms intent. To continue the same work, re-affirm with
# `eval "$(holly task resume)"`; to switch, re-run start with a new intent.
```

The `SessionStart` hook performs the boring bootstrap first: it resolves the
existing Holly session, initializes `agent-fs`, and, when no task is active,
posts an intent-only `<agent name> <session id> online and ready.` task. Treat
that as startup readiness only; switch to a real issue/worktree task before
editing files.

`HOLLY_SESSION_ID` is read-only identity. Holly commands may validate it, record
task state for it, and refresh activity; they do not create, export, repair, or
backfill it. `holly task start` requires the existing ID, records task ID and/or
intent, and changes to the project root or worktree when eval'd. `holly task
start --intent "<agent-intent>"` is the single intent-setter: it records the
task, posts intent to `all-agents`, and unblocks gated tools in one step.

Interactive shell startup may assign a terminal-local `HOLLY_SESSION_ID` so
concurrent VS Code/Codex terminals do not share one identity. Re-sourcing the
shell profile inside the same terminal should keep the existing value.

If Holly did not pick up the top-level shell's `HOLLY_SESSION_ID`
automatically, run:

```bash
holly session get
```

That's the recovery path. Do not inspect this through context-mode first; the
context-mode sandbox may not inherit the top-level shell environment.

When using context-mode, every `holly ...` command passed to `ctx_execute` or
`ctx_batch_execute` MUST start with the parent-shell prefix:
`HOLLY_SESSION_ID=$HOLLY_SESSION_ID holly ...`. The sandbox may not inherit
`HOLLY_SESSION_ID`; do not inspect only the sandbox env and report
`HOLLY_SESSION_ID is not set` until the same Holly command has been retried
with that prefix. If the prefix expands empty inside context-mode, resolve the
existing value in the real shell and pass it explicitly as
`HOLLY_SESSION_ID=agent-<existing-parent-shell-value> holly ...`.

If the hook reports an unclaimed VS Code UUID, run the exact `holly session ...`
command it prints. Do not call the private `session` shim directly.

After `HOLLY_SESSION_ID` is present and task/intent verification succeeds, gated
tools are unblocked. Tool visibility checks come after this bootstrap: do not
diagnose missing MCP or deferred tools until the session ID and task intent are
valid.

### VS Code Multi-root Workspaces

When VS Code opens multiple git repositories in one workspace, bootstrap
an **origin session** first, then add repo-local sessions only when you
need them. Non-repo folders borrow the origin session; they do not get
their own `.agent-sessions.json` file.

Default lazy bootstrap:

```bash
holly session workspace bootstrap \
  --name 'YourAgentName' \
  --uuid <your-vscode-uuid> \
  --task 'Description of what you are doing' \
  --intent 'Specific plan for this work'
```

That creates/claims the origin repo session only. If a later tool targets
another repo in the same VS Code workspace, the hook blocks with a repo
bootstrap hint. Run the targeted form:

```bash
holly session workspace bootstrap \
  --name 'YourAgentName' \
  --uuid <your-vscode-uuid> \
  --repo /path/to/target-repo \
  --task 'Description of what you are doing' \
  --intent 'Specific plan for this work'
```

If you know you will work across every repo in the workspace, use eager
bootstrap:

```bash
holly session workspace bootstrap \
  --name 'YourAgentName' \
  --uuid <your-vscode-uuid> \
  --all-repos \
  --task 'Description of what you are doing' \
  --intent 'Specific plan for this work'
```

Routing order for hook checks is target repo first, then origin repo,
then other workspace repos. A path inside a non-repo workspace folder
uses the origin session.

### Common Mistakes That Cause Deadlock

- Calling the private `session` shim directly instead of Holly lifecycle commands
- Treating Holly lifecycle commands as identity bootstrap; missing
  `HOLLY_SESSION_ID` is a blocker
- In a multi-root workspace, claiming the UUID in one shell/session store
  and then running tools from another; use `holly session workspace bootstrap`
  so each repo gets its own session store when needed
- Trying `read_file` or `grep_search` before claiming the UUID —
  these ARE exempt and should work, but if they don't, the hook
  may have a bug. Report it.

### What Each Step Does

| Step | Command                                                               | What It Does                                                        |
| ---- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 0    | `echo "$HOLLY_SESSION_ID"`                                             | Confirms shell/runtime-owned identity is present                    |
| 1    | `eval "$(holly task start --id <issue-id> --intent '<agent-intent>')"` | Records task ID / intent, posts intent to `all-agents`, unblocks tools |
| 2    | `eval "$(holly task resume)"` only if intent-sensitive tooling blocks or you need to re-enter the worktree | Re-affirms intent (`intent_posted=true`), re-enters your worktree, broadcasts pickup |
| 3    | `holly session claim-vscode-uuid <uuid>` if the hook asks                         | Maps your VS Code chat window's internal UUID to your Holly session |

