## Task Intent Management

After each new user prompt, the `UserPromptSubmit` hook classifies the prompt
with OpenAI (`gpt-5-nano`) against the current session intent. If the prompt
continues the same intent, the hook stays silent. If the prompt starts a new
intent, the hook updates `current_task.intent` automatically and keeps
`intent_posted=true`. If classification fails, the hook emits a warning but does
not reset intent.

Manual commands are now recovery/debug paths, not the normal per-turn loop:

### Commands

```bash
# Re-enter your active worktree or recover if intent-sensitive tooling blocks.
# Also broadcasts a "picked back up" notice to all-agents:
eval "$(holly task resume)"

# Switching to DIFFERENT work — post a new intent (add --id only if you will
# write to disk, which gives you a worktree):
holly task start --intent "<agent-intent>"
eval "$(holly task start --id <issue-id> --intent '<agent-intent>')"

# Debug-only low-level task state
holly task abort
```

### When to Re-affirm

- When you need to re-enter the active worktree after a shell reset
- If you see a BLOCKED message about intent not affirmed
- If the hook warns that automatic intent classification failed and a later
  intent-sensitive command blocks

### Quick Re-affirm Pattern

If you get blocked but your work hasn't changed, run `eval "$(holly task
resume)"`. It re-affirms intent, re-enters your worktree (if you have one), and
broadcasts a "picked back up" notice. Re-running `holly task start --id
<same-id>` also works and is idempotent — it re-posts intent and re-enters the
worktree without re-claiming the issue:

```bash
# Intent-only task (no worktree):
holly task start --intent "<agent-intent>"
# Worktree task (has an issue id):
eval "$(holly task start --id <issue-id> --intent '<agent-intent>')"
```

That's it whether or not the task changed — the same command records the task
ID and intent and posts intent in one step.

